      <nav class="breadcrumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Field guide</a><span aria-hidden="true">/</span><a href="/learn/">Learn</a><span aria-hidden="true">/</span><span aria-current="page">Scrobbling</span></nav>
      <p class="kicker">Definition</p>
      <h1>What is scrobbling?</h1>
      <p class="standfirst">A song ends and the player moves on. The scrobble is what remains: a durable record of the media and the time it happened.</p>

      <h2 id="example">One example</h2>
      <p>A music player sends a track and playback time to a listening-history service. The durable record is the scrobble. A temporary “now playing” message can describe the same track without entering history.</p>

      <h2 id="history">Where the word came from</h2>
      <p>Last.fm traces Audioscrobbler to 2002. Its retired submissions protocol later distinguished temporary now-playing state from historical submissions. The distinction remains useful beyond music because it separates a player's current state from a record that should survive.</p>
      <p class="source-note">Sources: <a href="https://blog.last.fm/2008/05/21/building-the-next-lastfm">Last.fm, “Building the Next Last.fm”</a> and the <a href="https://www.last.fm/api/submissions">retired submissions protocol</a>.</p>

      <h2 id="record">What a scrobble can preserve</h2>
      <dl class="field-notes">
        <div><dt>Media context</dt><dd>The work, release, episode, track, chapter or session known by the source.</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Event time</dt><dd>The time the record represents, distinguished from import or receipt time.</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Progress</dt><dd>The medium's native unit, when the source supplied one.</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Provenance</dt><dd>Manual entry, player observation, import, webhook, correction or synchronized copy.</dd></div>
      </dl>

      <h2 id="distinctions">What it is not</h2>
      <p>A watchlist records intent. A collection records ownership or availability. A rating records opinion. A scrobble records activity in history. A tracker can connect those records without storing them as the same fact.</p>

      <h2 id="media">Why media still differ</h2>
      <p>Listening often uses playback time. Reading may use pages or chapters. Episodic viewing needs an episode and order context. Games may be better represented by sessions than percentages. A shared exchange keeps those native facts instead of flattening them.</p>

      <h2 id="privacy">Why control matters</h2>
      <p>Media history can expose routines, beliefs and private interests. A useful tracker makes capture visible, allows correction and deletion, and provides an export without requiring unrelated device or location telemetry.</p>

